      "math_academy_id": "q-97940",
      "sequence_position": 7,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/e5138b92d20572b1680dfc010d6e0f8729de9c21c62448cdcb472efbf762c4d1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(-2x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=f(-(2x))\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=-f(-(2x)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/7d76ce80b4212d0cb6cd8b64c424492dd46cad3819ce3cba17bab22c4df115f5.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(-2x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/ded37839584d99725ab44ebfb4e60e05ea162da6a3845817aa96f6904d67146a.png)\n\n- Finally, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(-2x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/6f16d9eed1ed1b5fa41c46124f775238ecb759d79261cc66073c1fb304deb468.png)",
